  "id": "latherlye",
  "neon": "#8fe8ff",
  "note": "LATHER & LYE — the public baths, as the one HALL on a street of boxes. A municipal bathhouse is\na single vaulted volume, so it gets the barrel roof the warehouse set uses and nothing else on\nMarrow Street does; the frontage is pale glazed tile because that is what a building you hose down\nis faced in, and the roof is copper because the vault above a swimming hall sweats.\n\n⚠ THE WINDOWS ARE HIGH AND THERE IS NO SHOPFRONT, which is the whole tell. Every other frontage on\nthis street is glazed at eye level to sell you what is inside. A room full of naked people is\nglazed ABOVE head height and nowhere else, so the ground floor is a tiled wall with one door in it\nand the glazing is a clerestory band under the cornice. Getting that backwards would make it a\nshop.\n\n⚠ THE FLUES STAND BESIDE THE VAULT, NOT ON IT. Roof plant needs a DECK — a box with a real roof\nface — and a barrel has none; a stack placed on the vault would hang inside the curve. The main\nbox is 0.42 and the barrel is 0.30, which leaves a ledge of deck either side, and a pair of flues\non that ledge is also where a boiler house actually vents.\n\n⚠ AND THE BARREL RUNS ACROSS THE FRONT. `hl` is the half-length along the barrel's own axis and\n`hw` the half-width across it; laid the other way the street would see a long flat eave instead of\nthe gable, and the gable end is the half of a bath hall anybody recognises.",
  "pal": "ty_sw_milk",
  "replaces": "citybathhouse",
